{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/513011-how-important-is-arcadia","id":513011,"title":"How Important Is Arcadia?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Changeling: The Lost"],"posts":[{"date":"2015-05-13T09:51:52-07:00","id":513011,"title":"How Important Is Arcadia?","content":"Just an idle thought - how different would Changeling be if Arcadia... wasn't? Oh, the Hedge is there, and the Gentry may or may not be real... but Arcadia is nothing more than a fever dream, one that you can never return to?<br /> <br /> What difference would an absent Arcadia make to a game of Beautiful Madness?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I have decided, after some thought, that I don't really feel happy on these forums. I might decide to come back to post. Who knows - but right now, I'm gone.<br /> <br /> So good bye, good luck, and have a nice day.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1170-amechra","name":"amechra","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1170_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2316"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-13T10:25:31-07:00","id":513042,"title":"","content":"Huge.<br /> <br /> Like, monumental.<br /> <br /> Like, so big I don't actually have time right now to talk about it. But it's important.<br /> <br /> Of course, this isn't accounting for the fact that Arcadia is in many ways a fever dream that you may never return to, but for the sake of keeping this simple: Dreaming this ain't.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-13T12:38:00-07:00","id":513236,"title":"","content":"So congratulations. I should be doing homework of a time-sensitive nature, and beyond that I should be doing homework of a time-sensitive nature, and another one of not-as-sensitive-but-still nature, and beyond that I should be continuing my verbal ping-pong match of order of awesomeness with David on Gundam or contacting people for a game idea I'm trying to build, but NOPE. You've won the precedence by touching on a subject I'm been working over in my head, so I'm gonna respond to you in spite of that being a bad idea right now. This won't be a full essay-I'm saving that for when I finally decide to just brainbarf on the Hedge to get my thoughts straight-but you get a hefty bonus.<br /> <br /> *digs around for music to start on, settle for <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4GFDE-vdg98\" target=\"_blank\">Feels Like Falling by Digital Daggers</a>*<br /> <br /> Arcadia is the thing that tempts and repulses, that pulls and pushes on changelings, a core feature of the central struggles of Lost.<br /> <br /> Wait, wait, wait. No, I can't start there. I need to back up a second.<br /> <br /> Satchel once described Changeling the Lost, very aptly,as &quot;a game whose protagonists didn't want to completely abandon the routine and mundane.&quot; It's absolutely true-Arcadia is the <i>heights and depths</i> of experience within the mortal coil(and maybe even beyond in some ways). Changelings don't just run from trauma when they escape Arcadia, they escape endless wonder the likes of which they'll never really have, because they have chosen to prioritize the simpler, the quieter, the more mundane. Now, a large part of that comes from how the Lost tie the two together, and a big part of the recovery process when they get back as well as one of the big deciders of how a changeling decides to live their life comes from decoupling the trauma from the fae and allowing themselves to enjoy the goblin markets and the moonlight dances without slipping into the trained traits they picked up in surviving their time in Arcadia. There's an allure as well as a fear, and both can be as dangerous as they can be healthy(if there is such a thing as a healthy state of being for a changeling).<br /> <br /> I know, I know, Can't you substitute that with the Hedge? Can't you do that by having Changelings be haunted by their own dreams?And the answer is!<br /> <br /> No. Not really. And a large part of that has to do with what the Hedge stands in for and what Arcadia stands in for.<br /> <br /> Let me start off with a sort of visual contrast. The Hedge can be a pretty trippy place. It can be a thorned collage of office spaces(and Office Space), the 1893 Chicago World Fair, an underground kingdom, and Tony Stark's Happy Fun Fun Toyland of Technology. It can be these things because it represents the world slightly beyond the edge of what we see. When you got into the woods, you're leaving the security of what you know and into the wider world....but there's a buffer where the limits of what you know exceed the infinite variability of the strange and exotic. When you enter into the woods, you don't <i>just</i> drop off into the desert or into the Snow Queen's hive-castle or the labyrinths of either witch or goblin. This transition, this orphic journey into the subconscious, has challenges that make it distinct that must be passed before being passed, but I'll come back to this later. THe key point is that as strange as the Hedge can be, there's a reason it's limited to a certain expression of 'exotic worldliness', at least as of First Edition. And as such, there are things it can't be.<br /> <br /> See, the Hedge can't be cubist, or post-impressionist, or straight up <i>surrealism</i>. Arcadia, on the other hand, can. Arcadia is the end of the road and the extremes of human expression. You might have a maze in the Hedge, but in Arcadia you have Escher drawing the landscape and rules. Arcadia is everything great and terrible about human imagination turned up to eleven and caught in acts of intimacy that blend the two sides together like sex partners transcending themselves.It is terrifying and traumatizing, it is sensual and boundless, and each is appealing and rejecting in it's own right. It's <i>almost</i> nonsensical. It is the Gauntlet, the Tower of Revolution, the dark wellspring beneath the human family we enter to self-actualize and maybe even self-transcend in. The Hedge(by itself, not with outside help like the Strangers), prepares people to enter into this conflation of the widest world and most inner-self, to either fully accept it or reject it by challenging <i>everything</i>, taking every preconceived notion and asking &quot;Oh Really?&quot;. A person, be they human or changeling, if they follow the roads the right way and play the story out the right way, will arrive at Arcadia ready to <i>become</i>, whatever it is they will change into. All that's left is the last door to open, the last threshold to walk across.<br /> <br /> Stop, I hear you. I'm coming around to your question.<br /> <br /> So, first off, what is Arcadia was a dream, one you could never return to?<br /> <br /> The first problem with that thought is that it presupposes something contrary to Lost's themes, ideas, and struggles. The game begins with that pre-supposition that characters value mortality more so than the Arcadian wastes, that they take the muted over the vibrant. The conflict from the game stems from the then-growing balance of traumatic response and nostalgic recollection and pushes the tension/drama button of having your choice taken away from you-that you'll be forced into the wonderful and the traumatic without your choice, your control. Having Arcadia be a real place that you stand a real chance of returning to is bad enough for this sort of game, that potential subtle slide to glorious want and indulgence, and it becomes a twistier, scarier, and more interesting affair when Arcadia has the power to come after you. The conflation of these issues are what drive the game, create the story, set the tension.<br /> <br /> You <i>could</i> do that with the Hedge.....but the only way you could do it fully is by making the Hedge <i>more like Arcadia</i>.<br /> <br /> THe descent, the journey, the progress-it all lends to a natural segregation, and if you fought against that, it would lead to a dilution of the ideas. You ran from something terrible, and because it was no more yielding than a dream, when you struggle with what is worthwhile and what is now, all that's left is to wander the Hedge meaninglessly for the rest of your life. There's just nothing to twist the issue,the knife, nothing to make the character writhe with, nor is there any conclusion to that story. In the end, the Character <i>must</i> accept mortality, because there's nothing for them back in the world of their nostalgia. That can be an awesome story, but it becomes so much more awesome when it becomes a <i>choice</i> among a sea of viable outcomes rather than an inevitabilty. And the instant you start creating the choice, you by necessity create Arcadian Realms, because it is the Crucible where these choices are made manifest and whole. It becomes more than a dream, it becomes <i>laws</i>.<br /> <br /> Changeling the Dreaming is the game about dealing with an eternal loss of the great and wonderous that may also be a collection of torments, and it does it <i>extremely well, </i>mechanically(Well, okay, a lot of the machiancis need work, but there's some really good ones in there none the less) and idealistically set to explore the death of nostalgia and the birth of realization, and all things beautiful and terrible about the experience. <i>Dreaming</i> is the game about the careful balance of moving forward while hanging onto the past. But Lost isn't set to play that game. It explores the other side of the game, and the game would be crippled if you tried to balance many of the features and ideas inherent to the game while trying to take out Arcadia, because Arcadia is the central and final manifestation of the thousand complex progressions at work in Changeling the Lost.<br /> <br /> All paths lead to Arcadia, else they lead nowhere at all.<br /> <br /> I'm going to come back to this later and ask myself what I smoked from Dodgson's stores, so ask questions-you just might expose me as being full of shite.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":99,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","user":"ArcaneArts","date":". 05-13-2015, 03:52 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":8}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-13T15:20:33-07:00","id":513355,"title":"","content":"First, I think that our dear <b><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts\">ArcaneArts</a></b> started AND ended perfectly well:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>ArcaneArts</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/513011-how-important-is-arcadia?p=513236#post513236\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Arcadia is the thing that tempts and repulses, that pulls and pushes on changelings, a core feature of the central struggles of Lost.<br /> ...All paths lead to Arcadia, else they lead nowhere at all.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm of an opinion that no changeling really leaves Faerie/Arcadia/Hell/Heaven\u2014whatever one chooses to call it\u2014completely by choice. That is, every one of the Lost stumbles out of the Hedge with a piece of Arcadia stuck inside her forever. That splinter gives her the Changeling Template (in 'mechanics' speak), but it also acts as a temptation, one that she dare not voice to any other of the Lost. Thing is, each and every one of them feel the same way.<br /> <br /> Without this fundamental notion of Arcadia, the Hedge is still scary. Without using Arcadia, some version of the Others\u2014maybe hobs, other Lost, or whoever else populates the Hedge\u2014may still act as terrible threats. Without Arcadia, the game can still be played, but it's not the same game without Arcadia. The above treatise (NOT as essay, as <b>Arcane Arts</b> says!) wonderfully explains the need. I want to build on one piece of it, though, concerning <i>theme</i>:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>ArcaneArts</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/513011-how-important-is-arcadia?p=513236#post513236\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The conflict from the game stems from the then-growing balance of traumatic response and nostalgic recollection and pushes the tension/drama button of having your choice taken away from you-that you'll be forced into the wonderful and the traumatic without your choice, your control.</div> </div> </div> </div>This thematic piece of <b>Changeling: the Lost</b> resonates more that any other with my troupe. The Lost don't ever truly share any experiences in Arcadia, and even those who did serve Durances under the same Keeper will return with different scars, different nightmares, different memories. What they all DO share is that experience of having all control taken away, of having all choice robbed in a place that doesn't exist on any map, taken by a being that has no body to touch. In Arcadia, in their homes, the True Fae are as gods, at least when compared to a mortal's conception of such things. Within those realms, anything can, does, and did happen, but the game doesn't take place there. Why?<br /> <br /> This takes a little unpacking, but I'm gonna answer it.<br /> <br /> The <i>theme</i> of <b>Changeling: the Lost</b> that most appeals to me\u2014and is listed first in the book\u2014involves the notion of running <i>towards</i> something that might be more than running <i>away</i> from anything known. No matter how happy Lochen of Redleaf was in her Keeper's realm, no matter how powerful and hardy she felt, no matter the adoration heaped upon her, she had no power to choose her destiny. Life out here in the 'real' world requires a job, rent payments, stinky commute on the tube and the rain every <i>bloody</i> day... But it is HER life. That monster in his palace of glass and pure joy has no say over what Lochen\u2014now 'Lauren' on the cheques she writes\u2014does with her own time or energy. Sure, the pampering and the perfect weather was nice, but that wasn't her life.<br /> <br /> Our changeling characters maybe DO run from the horrors of the Hedge and Arcadia, but the thematic meat of the game is that each and every one of them seeks acceptance, home, a place to be who he wants to be. If Arcadia were closer (i.e. just the Hedge), that struggle would maybe gain something in terms of immediacy or 'lookoutbehindyou' sort of scares, but it would lose the life-shattering, pants-shitting, sanity-breaking POWER of the True Fae in their own realms of Arcadia.<br /> <br /> ...So yeah, I reckon that I partially just regurgitated a bit of what <b>ArcaneArts</b> wanted to 'brainbarf' on us (brilliant term!). That's not the point. My ACTUAL point is that I really don't see the above theme very well supported in what I've seen of Second Edition so far. I mean, the ONLY 'mechanical' hindrance laid on a starting character is her kith, which comes with a decently kick-ass Blessing and yeah, a weird shape that the Mask covers, hence not much of a hindrance. Seeming is chosen after her escape and comes with a 'curse' I don't quite understand yet\u2014still awaiting information on how Clarity will work\u2014and another Blessing. Then she gets to choose a Court, possibly an Entitlement, her Contracts (possibly more 'a-la-carte' than linear?), and that is a LOT of <i>character</i> (NOT player, mind you) agency involved. Throw in the nigh omnipresent threat of these 'Huntsmen' and we wind up with a version of <b>Changeling</b> based a lot more on running away and using powers to keep the 'bad guy' (the True Fae, hey?) from getting you. Between those two, it's a lot of serious change in theme I think.<br /> <br /> Please know that *I* know that I'm way off topic here, but I needed this space to get my notions in order. Sorry all...<br /> <br /> A little more on topic, my better half told me after our last game: 'I think it's the very notion of HOPE that makes this game so scary.' We'd spent hours making characters with some pick-and-choose elements from First Edition and kiths modeled after Second Edition, all jumbled with some 'homebrew' Anchors and a slight change in Clarity that I'd crafted for years. The process of defining character didn't use a LOT of Arcadia stuff (I'm super-willing to let those details come out later in play), but just <i>knowing</i> that a horror beyond mortal understanding and beyond the mortal realms forged these characters into something that won't ever QUITE be human again, <i>knowing</i> that the world in which these characters live must be chosen and built from the ashes of that experience... Oh, that gives these characters something precious that they dare not let slip away. That hope and the certainty that it could all be taken away, THAT is the horror I so adore in <b>Changeling</b>!<br /> <br /> To answer my question from above: It cannot take place in Arcadia, but the experiences in THIS world wouldn't carry the same contrast between hope and dread, freedom and captivity, chaos and order without Arcadia as a feature. Disagree in whole or in part, but that's honestly how I feel about it.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>B.I.G. Bird spreads the word: Anybody with a heart votes love<br /> <a href=\"https://absintheandthorns.obsidianportal.com/\" target=\"_blank\">https://absintheandthorns.obsidianportal.com/</a></i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/409-phaolan","name":"Phaolan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar409_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"478"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-13T15:39:17-07:00","id":513369,"title":"","content":"Second Edition is a very different (which is not to say worse) game, especially compared to others. I expect there will be more people playing different editions of Changeling than other games. Of course, I also expect some stealing of good stuff both ways as well.<br /> <br /> Beyond that, both sets of comments seem very on the mark, I have little else to add other than good job.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/OnyxGraphics/OnyxMod.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Onyx Path Moderator </span><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/onyx-path-announcements/206-forum-rules\" target=\"_blank\">Forum Rules</a><br /> <span style=\"color:#FF0000\">This is my mod voice.</span> This is my goth voice.<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/geist-the-sin-eaters/15731-geist-balance-of-shadows-actual-play\" target=\"_blank\">[Geist: Balance of Shadows</a><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/17537-v20-the-conspiracy-of-hrad-%C4%8Dern%C3%A1-hora-actual-play\" target=\"_blank\"> ][ Vampire: The Conspiracy of Hrad \u010cern\u00e1 Hora</a><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/13838-scion-bohemian-front-actual-play\" target=\"_blank\"> ][ Scion: Bohemian Front</a>][<a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/99033-changeling-malibu-dream-house-actual-play\" target=\"_blank\">Changeling: Malibu Dream House</a>] [<a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/demon-the-descent/268069-demon-night-train-detective-agency-actual-play\" target=\"_blank\">Demon: Night Train Detective Agency</a>] <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/la94fvk\" target=\"_blank\">[WoD: The Golden Eagle</a>]","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/56-baroness-nerak","name":"Baroness Nerak","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar56_2.jpeg","title":"Moderator","rank":6,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2524"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-13T16:08:11-07:00","id":513383,"title":"","content":"Excellent.<br /> <br /> Thanks for the in-depth responses, everyone.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I have decided, after some thought, that I don't really feel happy on these forums. I might decide to come back to post. Who knows - but right now, I'm gone.<br /> <br /> So good bye, good luck, and have a nice day.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1170-amechra","name":"amechra","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1170_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2316"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-13T17:06:56-07:00","id":513476,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Phaolan</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/513011-how-important-is-arcadia?p=513355#post513355\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> My ACTUAL point is that I really don't see the above theme very well supported in what I've seen of Second Edition so far..</div> </div> </div> </div>*cough cough* Strictly speaking, I think the direction of Second Edition actually helps to re-emphasize a lot of what's discussed. Changeling ran some risk of losing sight of sharp horror in the first Edition, which is coming back in Second Edition, but the themes of reclamation-or even claiming-still run strong. The risks are tighter, but the down and out psycho-spiritual bloodying for self-defined identity and personal sense of...not security, but comfort is still front and center.<br /> <br /> I think the main thing is an emphasis that a Changeling has reason to always be afraid, and at the end of the day it's finding a way to get beyond that in a number of ways.<br /> <br /> And by the way, I love that summation: &quot;I think it's the very notion of HOPE that makes this game so scary.&quot; Changeling is not a fatalistic or nihilistic game. Hope haunts them as much as trauma does. Wishes, dreams, happy endings-changelings can <i>grasp these things</i>, and they can be just as wonderful and terrible as anything a Stranger can put down. Hope can be scary for a changeling-but on the more appropriate level, Changeling maintains its' horror because it maintains its' hope as well, with both being completely possible destinations as well as paths.<br /> <br /> PS: This was not the brainbarf. I've been wanting to sit down and write an essay on the Hedge, brainbarfing as I unravel it and weave it back together to just get a deeper understanding of it, but I've been busy.<br /> <br /> Glad you enjoy the term, by the way.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-23T12:41:27-07:00","id":525787,"title":"","content":"Without Arcadia, the cosmology of the game makes no sense. The Hedge only exists because there must be a buffer zone between Earth and Arcadia. No Arcadia means no Hedge. No Arcadia also means nowhere for the Gentry to exist, unless you want to place them somewhere in the Astral, which wreaks havoc on Mage. and Changeling. Never mind the thematics and the symbolism (Arcane Arts covered that already), Arcadia cannot be removed and still have a coherent setting short of rewriting it, along with much of the changelings' powers and abilities (and kiths and seemings into the bargain).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1184-ladylens","name":"LadyLens","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1132"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-23T12:46:26-07:00","id":525797,"title":"","content":"You could still have the Hedge, but you'd have to re-engineer a lot.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-23T22:10:49-07:00","id":526392,"title":"","content":"If Arcadia is &quot;a fever dream, one you can never return to&quot;, does that mean that Changelings still remember suffering a Durance with a Keeper in Arcadia in your hypothetical setting?<br /> <br /> If the Gentry &quot;may or may not be real&quot; who do the Changelings fear? Hobs? Other Changelings? Are there still Privateers, and, if so, who do they sell their victims to?<br /> <br /> If Arcadia is inaccessible and there is no hard evidence the Gentry are real, are there groups who believe they exist and groups that do not? How do the Arcadian skeptics explain memories of Durances or the existence of Changelings? What are the alternate theories? Are there groups of obsessed Zealots who search for ways to reach Arcadia or reach out to the Fair Folk? Do some believers try to convince other Changelings to behave in certain ways to avoid drawing the Gentry's attention? Are sone believers bridge burners? What do the other denizens of the hedge believe?<br /> <br /> Maybe the Gentry do exist, but in hidden places deep inside the hedge instead of Arcadia. Perhaps truly fantastic Durances are a result of the sanity stripping presence of the Fae and have little to do with any actual events. Or maybe there are no gentry, and memories of the Durance are actually a sort of communion with the weird, a torturous dream vision quest that exists only in the mind and results in the transformation into a Changeling?<br /> <br /> I think it could be very interesting, but it would definitely be a major change to the setting and themes. It's a huge impact, but it could be done and might even be pretty cool if done well.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5910-csschulz","name":"CSSchulz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5910_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"177"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-23T22:46:08-07:00","id":526416,"title":"","content":"As an aside, that Harmony-esque Fae-based Integrity stat people keep going on about could fit pretty well in such a radically shifted setting.<br /> <br /> My main problem is that the more I think about it, the more I go &quot;This is Dreaming painted in Lost colors.&quot; Not a bad thing in some ways, but I feel like I should just make the jump straight to Dreaming.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-23T23:48:25-07:00","id":526432,"title":"","content":"I think it's still a far cry from Dreaming. Arcadia may be off limits in Dreaming, but it's definitely real. The True Fae could refer to a bunch of different things in CtD, including Denizens, Inanime, and even the player characters' fae souls before undergoing the Changeling way, most of which are pretty well known to actually exist. The Near Dreaming functions very differently from the Hedge. Hell, the types of things that you describe as only being possible in Arcadia in CtL (and therefore not possible at all in a game with only the Hedge) could very well exist in the Far or Deep Dreaming. And there are a ton of things that just aren't present at all from Dreaming: the Tuatha and their fight with the Fomorians, the Denizens, the Autumn people, the Sidhe as a ruling class, the fading dreaming, and the seemingly inevitable &quot;winter&quot; to come. Those are major themes and elements. Without any of that stuff, The Dreaming it ain't. Sure it would be different from standard CtL if you ran the world with an absent Arcadia (and/or Harmony-2e-like Integrity stat). Maybe very different, depending on how heavily you use those themes in your games. But I don't think it would be much like Dreaming, personally.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5910-csschulz","name":"CSSchulz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5910_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"177"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-25T11:51:30-07:00","id":527921,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>amechra</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/513011-how-important-is-arcadia#post513011\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Just an idle thought - how different would Changeling be if Arcadia... wasn't? Oh, the Hedge is there, and the Gentry may or may not be real... but Arcadia is nothing more than a fever dream, one that you can never return to?<br /> <br /> What difference would an absent Arcadia make to a game of Beautiful Madness?</div> </div> </div> </div> To me, not much. I personally always ran things as if &quot;Arcadia&quot; was just certain areas in the Hedge shaped by powerful Fae to their whims. <br /> <br /> Arcadia, itself, is not important. The trip away from a powerful Keeper through the Thorns is... and all that can be done with just the Hedge itself, shaped by dreams and a powerful will. Some people call this re-engineering it... I don't think it is. There's already hints of it, from what we know of it, in the Hedge of 1e. Arcadia is just more of the same thing, which can be accounted for by powerful wills imposing over the landscape.<br /> <br /> Changeling starts off in a different manner - you start off as not a victim, but a survivor. You escaped, you beat them once. Then, you come home to rebuild your life. What are the important parts of the story here? Taken away by a powerful Keeper, changed/abused, and then the victorious escape back. Then game starts with learning to cope. The Keeper, the abuse and what they made you do (2e kith), and the escape (2e Seeming) are the important parts here, not the actual location.<br /> <br /> When you get right down to it, you could engineer the game so that there's no Hedge OR Arcadia, but little pockets of hidden reality akin to what the God-Machine does. Getting rid of the Hedge would require a bit more tweaking, however, given how involved it is in Changeling by default, but Arcadia itself? Not important. Remember, most changelings fully remember their experience in the hedge in 2e - its not a fever dream, except for those poor unfortunates that spent their time as unthinking animals, plants or objects (which is because not being humanoid is an issue).<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2102-mcn","name":"MCN","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2102_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3479"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-25T13:02:35-07:00","id":528029,"title":"","content":"I have to agree with MCN. Most changeling games won't really have anything to do with Arcadia, beyond the background. Allowing it to remain as some ephemeral dream that doesn't exist beyond the existence of the changeling and the looming possibility of their Keeper, wouldn't really break the game.<br /> <br /> But cutting it out entirely would limit your 'endgame' potential.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-25T13:20:06-07:00","id":528041,"title":"","content":"The hobbits weren't in Mordor until the third book. Just saying.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-26T07:15:11-07:00","id":528769,"title":"","content":"Mordor isn't LotR's Arcadia. Its the one realm that all the elves as well as Frodo and Bilbo sailed off to at the end of the story after reclaiming the Shire. <br /> <br /> If you're just going to associate &quot;where the Big Bad hangs out&quot; as Faerie, then we have to say that Mirkwood (Sauron chilled there for a long while) or the dwarf mountain where Smaug hung out were parts of Faerie too... which is problematic to say the least.<br /> <br /> <br /> The funny thing about calling Arcadia a &quot;fever dream&quot; that really amuses me, though, is that there is an undeniable link between dreams, the Hedge, and Faerie. For all we know, Faerie could be a realm made of entirely dreams as it is. <br /> <br /> <br /> End game potential? Putting aside that most tables don't use Game of Immortals as a natural extension of the plot, you could just say that getting a Title lets you Hedgeshape an area to suit your fancy to a far greater extent.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2102-mcn","name":"MCN","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2102_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3479"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-26T07:20:37-07:00","id":528772,"title":"","content":"No, it's not LotR's Arcadia.<br /> <br /> <i>It's Frodo's and Sam's Arcadia</i>.<br /> <br /> Look at narrative functions.<br /> <br /> EDIT: And yes, the Lonely Mountain is also the Arcadia of the Hobbit. It is the place of greatest transformative potential for all the characters who go there.<br /> <br /> EDIT: Also, in a traditional narrative, Mirkwood would also be Arcadia, since that is the point where Bilbo fully becomes a trickster hero. But it's the Hobbit, and everything from Smaug's sharp reminder about life beyond the adventure to the war is how Bilbo takes what he's gotten and transforms again into what he needs to be, that being a hobbit <i>with perspective</i>.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":99,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","user":"ArcaneArts","date":". 05-26-2015, 11:11 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-26T10:08:46-07:00","id":528908,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>ArcaneArts</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/513011-how-important-is-arcadia?p=528772#post528772\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">No, it's not LotR's Arcadia.<br /> <br /> <i>It's Frodo's and Sam's Arcadia</i>.<br /> <br /> Look at narrative functions.<br /> <br /> EDIT: And yes, the Lonely Mountain is also the Arcadia of the Hobbit. It is the place of greatest transformative potential for all the characters who go there.<br /> <br /> EDIT: Also, in a traditional narrative, Mirkwood would also be Arcadia, since that is the point where Bilbo fully becomes a trickster hero. But it's the Hobbit, and everything from Smaug's sharp reminder about life beyond the adventure to the war is how Bilbo takes what he's gotten and transforms again into what he needs to be, that being a hobbit <i>with perspective</i>.</div> </div> </div> </div>Isn't Mirkwood Bilbo's Hedge, by that definition?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-26T10:23:46-07:00","id":528921,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Charlaquin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/513011-how-important-is-arcadia?p=528908#post528908\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Isn't Mirkwood Bilbo's Hedge, by that definition?</div> </div> </div> </div>Hell yes.<br /> <br /> I have more I wanna say using the Hobbit(And Labyrinth and Pan's Labyrinth and American McGee's Alice and Flight of Dragons) as a model for Changeling the Lost, but that's for later in the day. But yes, Mirkwood is definitely the Hedge, the Hedge before Arcadia even(No surprise that this amazingly formative moment is literally the last thing before they reach the Lonely Mountain region). It's not where Bilbo transcends the story that's been laid before him and completely self-actualizes, but he is now mythic, a true peer to the dwarves, and is at the height of the adventurous spirit. He almost loses sight of himself, in a way, at this point, being the most 'worldly' creature he'll ever be.<br /> <br /> Fortunately, Smaug, the self-consuming tale, challenges him with what lies beyond the story, and that very quickly snaps him back into perspective as he finds the balance between trickster hero who knows things now and the simple hobbit who knew things the world forgot, culminating in his trading of the Arkenstone.<br /> <br /> But more on that later today. I've got videos to edit.<br /> <br /> EDIT: I mean, fairness, everything from outside of the Shire to Laketown is the Hedge, but Mirkwood is the culminating point of the Hedge, the part that epitomizes something of what the Hedge does and what it stands in for.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":99,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","user":"ArcaneArts","date":". 05-26-2015, 02:19 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-26T20:51:31-07:00","id":529573,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>ArcaneArts</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/513011-how-important-is-arcadia?p=528772#post528772\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">No, it's not LotR's Arcadia.<br /> <br /> <i>It's Frodo's and Sam's Arcadia</i>.<br /> <br /> Look at narrative functions.<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>That's the thing though; any place can fit that narrative function. The place that fills that role in your chronicle could be a forbidden place deep in the Hedge or even someplace in the mortal realm, depending on the story. Arcadia can do it very well in many instances, but it doesn't need to be Arcadia.<br /> <br /> I think making the existence of the Gentry uncertain is more game-changing than removing Arcadia. If the Keepers might not exist you have to rethink the origins and motivations of the Changelings and most of the antagonists. Why have Courts and Freeholds for mutual protection if the Gentry might not even be real? If Changelings can't say for certain that Keepers are real, where the hell do Fetches come from? And on and on. You'd have to tweak a lot.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5910-csschulz","name":"CSSchulz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5910_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"177"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-26T21:37:19-07:00","id":529589,"title":"","content":"Not....<i>quite</i> any place.<br /> <br /> There's an element of retreat to the True Fae as self-perpetuating stories. In a game about that is very much about smashing the egg of narrative to confront the reality beyond the shell(alongside several other things), The Strangers are the ones who cocoon most of all, willing to live in a fantasy because dealing with a world where the laws are surprisingly harsher is something of a terrifying notion. This is supported more in Second Edition where the Huntsmen fill in a lot more of the traditional roles.<br /> <br /> Arcadia is a metaphor that goes several ways. For changelings, it is the Tower of Revolution, the place where they can finally reject all the fantasies and systems that have been placed on them in favor of reality and freely engaging in that reality. For the Strangers, the final antithesis of the title character*, it is the deep dive, the total summation of a retreat into defined behaviors.<br /> <br /> It's not that I think that Arcadia is independently worthy of existence on it's own, but it is a natural by-product of where the themes and ideas (which, by the way, are many and layered and <i>holy shit just how far does this rabbit hole go</i>) progress. It's more unavoidable than anything else, something that will happen if you indulge in the conversation. <br /> <br /> As for the Strangers....well, like I said. They are the final antithesis to the player character. They embody everything counter to the ideals. There's no real side-stepping them in this game.<br /> <br /> Man, I get the feeling this is going to be going on for a long ass while. I'm half-tempted to just say &quot;I'll never be convinced&quot; and leave it at that, but I've got a few more thesises (Thesii?) I want to explore. If for no other reason than the fact I'm convinced this is how I'll get hired.<br /> <br /> *I'd need to see them to be sure, but I'm placing money on the progression of Fetch, Huntsman, and Stranger eerily reflecting, if not psychology, then certain beats of narrative theory.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-27T06:27:50-07:00","id":529862,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>ArcaneArts</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/513011-how-important-is-arcadia?p=528772#post528772\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">No, it's not LotR's Arcadia.<br /> <br /> <i>It's Frodo's and Sam's Arcadia</i>.<br /> <br /> Look at narrative functions.</div> </div> </div> </div> I have. I just flat out disagree with you. Not only that &quot;BBEG lair&quot; should not equal &quot;Arcadia,&quot; but &quot;place of transformative potential&quot; isn't always the Keeper's home either. One of Frodo's biggest &quot;places of transformative potential&quot; happened in the wilderness when he was stabbed with the knife and put on the ring for the first time. Or, alternatively if you wish, at Elrond's council when he accepts the burden of the ring, which is the narrative equivalent to a local Court's hallow - definitely not something like Arcadia. Another huge, transformative moment is when the Fellowship disbanded... in the middle of the wilderness / Hedge.<br /> <br /> Further, if we extend this metaphor, I would argue that Sauron should be equated to an Exiled Fae, specifically cut off from the beautiful madness of Arcadia and twisting things in reality as a result. Not every CtL story reaches its full potential by traveling through Arcadia to confront the Gentry. In fact, in my experience, its a smaller minority of games that conclude that way.<br /> <br /> I also find the idea of &quot;Bilbo's Arcadia&quot; and &quot;Frodo / Sam's Arcadia&quot; or &quot;Merry's Arcadia&quot; to be very... odd. If anything, that suggests that Arcadia is subjective enough to be a personal dream and not an objective reality.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>CSSchulz</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/513011-how-important-is-arcadia?p=529573#post529573\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">That's the thing though; any place can fit that narrative function. The place that fills that role in your chronicle could be a forbidden place deep in the Hedge or even someplace in the mortal realm, depending on the story. Arcadia can do it very well in many instances, but it doesn't need to be Arcadia.</div> </div> </div> </div> I can agree here, especially with the reactive/reshaping nature of the Hedge to emotion, will, and story potential.<br /> <br /> It feels very odd to me to call any place that has a transformative narrative function &quot;Arcadia&quot; when that label applies to vast number of plot points and locations. Maybe not <i>anywhere</i>, but if the location is flexible, then we're not really talking Arcadia as CtL describes it anymore, I think.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>ArcaneArts</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/513011-how-important-is-arcadia?p=529589#post529589\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Not....<i>quite</i> any place.</div> </div> </div> </div> But a vast number of places, yes? In CtL, Arcadia is a specific place you travel to via the Hedge. You can't have one spot be one Lost's Arcadia, and a second spot in the Hedge be another Arcadia for some, but not others.<br /> <br /> You're using a lot of meta-level metaphors for the path of the story and everything, but lets bring this back down a few notches, shall we? Arcadia, in CtL, is a specific place, and serves a specific role in the story of the game. Where the Keepers live, and the 'lings used and abused in various ways, and where they escaped from. The problem is? Where most stories would have that as the ending, escaping the BBEG, its where CtL begins. You've escaped, and a new story is beginning.<br /> <br /> So, what story role does Arcadia have other than &quot;home of the True Fae?&quot; Because, if that's all it is, then Arcadia is<br /> <br /> Now, the WoD is pretty big on making metaphors literal. In PtC, the spiritual journey a prommie goes on is also a physical Pilgrimage. In VtR, the titular characters are hurt by light and flame because they're both literally and figuratively creatures of darkness. This applies to CtL as well - they are both spiritually Lost, as well as physically Lost, looking to find their way home. Its notable that the Lost don't have a &quot;safe place&quot; equivalent in the real world, only the Hedge. Their home hasn't been established.<br /> <br /> So, Arcadia. Its a transformative place, yes, but all due to the Keeper. Its where you get your physical/spiritual scars, where you're literally and metaphorically reduced to something less than human. You're being abused on so many levels, and in so many potential ways. Its where you fight back in some way, and escape.<br /> <br /> I do think that confronting the abuser is a natural part of a good number of stories. I think that the real threat of being put back under the abuser's thumb is a natural part of the story, yes. Or becoming the abuser. Does all this need to be in Arcadia? No - people confront their abusers in lots of places, not the abuser's place of power. Once you escape the Other's &quot;place of power&quot; do we need to ever return? No, remember we're talking about metaphors becoming literal - abused in reality might use the place as a stand in for confronting the abuser, but in this game, we want to have the confrontation be literal.<br /> <br /> So, with all this, Arcadia effectively becomes a backdrop. You never need to return there. For some stories, its appropriate to do it. For every story where its a necessity? No, cutting it out won't hurt it. Cutting out the Gentry would be detrimental, but the place of power isn't.<br /> <br /> I should also point out that, while confronting an abuser is a powerful tool for many abused, it is NOT a universal cure. Not every person benefits from that, so not every story should travel that way. Arcadia loses power and meaning after the escape in such stories, rendering it even further into the &quot;never return to&quot; file.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> It's not that I think that Arcadia is independently worthy of existence on it's own, but it is a natural by-product of where the themes and ideas (which, by the way, are many and layered and <i>holy shit just how far does this rabbit hole go</i>) progress. It's more unavoidable than anything else, something that will happen if you indulge in the conversation. </div> </div> </div> Okay, let me stop you there. You just said here that Arcadia is not necessary.<br /> <br /> You are saying that you need all these themes and ideas into the story. I agree, those elements are key to a good story. But that where those elements are absolute must be Arcadia? That's something I think is shaky.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> As for the Strangers....well, like I said. They are the final antithesis to the player character. They embody everything counter to the ideals. There's no real side-stepping them in this game. </div> </div> </div>No one said to sidestep the Fae. This and that are separate issues.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Man, I get the feeling this is going to be going on for a long ass while. I'm half-tempted to just say &quot;I'll never be convinced&quot; and leave it at that, but I've got a few more thesises (Thesii?) I want to explore. </div> </div> </div> Why are we arguing a narrative thesis, anyways? We're gamers - writers need to write to their audience, people who want to play the game and tell a story, not dissect this like literature majors. We should be looking at this like a book club since this is written for hobbyists, shouldn't we?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2102,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2102-mcn","user":"MCN","date":". 05-27-2015, 09:32 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2102-mcn","name":"MCN","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2102_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3479"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-27T07:05:09-07:00","id":529898,"title":"","content":"I'm gonna respond to this proper after HTML/CSS is done, but I need this out there.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MCN</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/513011-how-important-is-arcadia?p=529862#post529862\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Why are we arguing a narrative thesis, anyways? We're gamers - writers need to write to their audience, people who want to play the game and tell a story, not dissect this like literature majors. We should be looking at this like a book club since this is written for hobbyists, shouldn't we?</div> </div> </div> </div>Probably because I'm an English Major with an emphasis on Creative Writing with a minor in Visual Communication.<br /> <br /> I probably make a ton of sense now.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-27T09:17:03-07:00","id":530030,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MCN</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/513011-how-important-is-arcadia?p=529862#post529862\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I also find the idea of &quot;Bilbo's Arcadia&quot; and &quot;Frodo / Sam's Arcadia&quot; or &quot;Merry's Arcadia&quot; to be very... odd. If anything, that suggests that Arcadia is subjective enough to be a personal dream and not an objective reality.</div> </div> </div> </div>A place whose only operative constants are its mutability and its inhabitance by one of many beings of godlike power for whom time and distance are largely inconveniences for the outside world is not what I would call &quot;objective reality&quot; in the first place.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> It feels very odd to me to call any place that has a transformative narrative function &quot;Arcadia&quot; when that label applies to vast number of plot points and locations. Maybe not <i>anywhere</i>, but if the location is flexible, then we're not really talking Arcadia as CtL describes it anymore, I think. </div> </div> </div>Arcadia is literally infinitely variable and marks the point in the metaphysics where a human being can be infused with the energies of the Wyrd to a sufficient degree to become a changeling.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> But a vast number of places, yes? In CtL, Arcadia is a specific place you travel to via the Hedge. You can't have one spot be one Lost's Arcadia, and a second spot in the Hedge be another Arcadia for some, but not others. </div> </div> </div>When you point to shaping the Hedge as a reason Arcadia is unnecessary and then ignore the effect the Lost's personal Wyrd has on shaping the Hedge and the nature of a Durance and all the explicit and unspoken qualifiers about what makes a person capable of taking on a particular kith, you are being either disingenuous or ridiculously myopic.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> So, Arcadia. Its a transformative place, yes, but all due to the Keeper. </div> </div> </div>The Gentry are only secondarily responsible for the fact that truth and inner nature are mutable in Arcadia.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> No one said to sidestep the Fae. This and that are separate issues. </div> </div> </div>The Fae <i>are Faerie</i>, and thus any talk about changing the ontology of Arcadia must necessarily address their handling as well.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-27T09:34:39-07:00","id":530056,"title":"","content":"THere is something to be said for Arcadia when one of the defining factors of the Strangers is that they are chicks that hatch into eggs.<br /> <br /> Also, thank you Satchel.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-27T14:27:13-07:00","id":530316,"title":"","content":"Still don't...quite feel like responding to the major stuff, but I want to hit on something.<br /> <br /> Why metafiction, and why narrative theory, for this discussion?<br /> <br /> Changeling is a thematically multilayered game, but still:<br /> <br /> 1) This is still a storytelling game. While the storytelling aspect does not override the game, it is still a factor in it. The World of Darkness keeps a loose sense of narrative function for their elements, applying storytelling craft to gameplay. This is more important for Changeling (and Beast) than the others, because<br /> <br /> 2) This is a game about stories as much as anything else. It unravels storytelling and it's craft to examine it closely and challenge it, and uses it as a parallel to psychological horror and drama, ala Jung. It draws its' pedigree as much from Alan Wake and Princess Tutu as much as it does Grimm and Anderson. The True Fae <i>are</i> self-perpetuating stories. The Wyrd <i>is</i> essentially the force of narrative inspiration. They aren't <i>just</i> these things, but a lot of the horror of Changeling has to do with the horror of the conventions we take for granted in fiction, particularly fantasy and especially faerie tales. It blends this real world fears and problems and grounds itself out with logical conclusions, but it's involved enough that any discussion about Lost needs to keep in mind that we're talking about our relationship with fiction on top of everything else. Understanding the broad and many functions from a literary standpoint gives us a framework to understand everything else with clarity.<br /> <br /> I had more points when I started, but I lost them. God I'm tired.<br /> <br /> Unrelated Point: TREATISE! That was the word I was looking for, not thesis.<br /> <br /> Words are arbitrary, language is a lie, and English is god-awfully, lousy, and full of umlauts.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":99,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","user":"ArcaneArts","date":". 05-27-2015, 06:26 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-27T21:25:17-07:00","id":530643,"title":"","content":"<span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">To be absolutely clear, when I said that any place can fit that narrative function, I meant that strictly in the Vladimir-Propp-style-narratology sense, since I assumed that's what Arcane meant. Narrative functions in that context are extremely general, such as &quot;hero leaves home&quot; or &quot;villain is defeated&quot;, so virtually any location can serve as the setting for any narrative function.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">Insomuch as Mordor or the Lonely Mountain can be the equivalent of Arcadia, because of the characters' actions or development there and how that moves the narrative forward, then any place can be made into the equivalent of Arcadia. That location doesn't need to have the same metaphysical characteristics or symbolic meanings to fulfill the same basic narrative function. Sure it won't do it in nearly the same manner as Arcadia, but neither does Mordor. And not every Changeling game will use Arcadia for that purpose. It's narrative function is going to be different in each Changeling's individual story (I'm talking post Durance here, it obviously fills the same role for each Changeling's backstory), and that function is separate from (though it could be influenced by) Arcadia's place in the setting's metaphysics and its metaphorical significance.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">I think the disagreement people are having is rooted in looking at the question &quot;How important is Arcadia?&quot; two different ways:</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">1. How important is Arcadia <i>to the canon</i>? - I think everyone can agree that Arcadia is a major part of the lore of the setting as written. Apart from the difference of its absence, removing it will cause ripples that will affect many aspects of the game. Lots of questions that will have to be answered for the setting to make sense, or that will become mysteries (see my previous posts). Additionally, it will remove or alter certain thematic elements of the game that some people consider core to the games identity. (Personally, I think that other things could be used in its place to restore most of those thematic elements, but that's still more change.)</span></span></span><br /> <br /> As opposed to -<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">2. How important is Arcadia <i>to running the game</i>? - This is going to be different from ST to ST and game group to game group. There are a lot of ideas and themes presented in CtL, and even more that suggest themselves; not every gaming group is going to use all of them. Some groups will focus on certain themes and let others drift into the background, some groups will largely ignore the setting's more prominent themes and replace them with themes of their own, and others won't worry about themes or metaphors or any of that at all and just enjoy the setting without analyzing it - it is a game, after all. So, how important it is to running a game depends on how heavily you tend to focus on the elements that will be affected the most by Arcadia's absence. I think this is where MCN is coming from. Not using every theme or idea that the material provides - or avoiding the &quot;central&quot; themes and focusing on &quot;lesser&quot; ones or ignoring the stated themes altogether to tell a story about themes that you find more interesting or important - doesn't make a game bad.<br /> <br /> Personally, I think a game with no Arcadia, or an unreachable Arcadia, could be an interesting way to explore new themes or to use different metaphors and elements to explore old themes. I think it would take a good bit of work to do it well, and it would definitely be a different experience, but it could be very rewarding. That said, I'll probably never get around to it...</span></span></span><br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5910-csschulz","name":"CSSchulz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5910_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"177"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-30T14:22:10-07:00","id":533643,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Satchel</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/513011-how-important-is-arcadia?p=530030#post530030\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">A place whose only operative constants are its mutability and its inhabitance by one of many beings of godlike power for whom time and distance are largely inconveniences for the outside world is not what I would call &quot;objective reality&quot; in the first place.</div> </div> </div> </div> That's taking what I said out of context. Arcadia is most definitely portrayed as a specific place you travel to. One area of the Hedge cannot be &quot;Arcadia&quot; for Tom Riddle the Summer Beast, while not Arcadia Jem Synergy the Spring Fairest singer. If an area is Arcadia, its Arcadia for everyone. That's what I mean by objective.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Arcadia is literally infinitely variable and marks the point in the metaphysics where a human being can be infused with the energies of the Wyrd to a sufficient degree to become a changeling. </div> </div> </div> Care to back that up with actual source material? That sounds like pure speculation to me.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> When you point to shaping the Hedge as a reason Arcadia is unnecessary and then ignore the effect the Lost's personal Wyrd has on shaping the Hedge and the nature of a Durance and all the explicit and unspoken qualifiers about what makes a person capable of taking on a particular kith, you are being either disingenuous or ridiculously myopic. </div> </div> </div> No, I brought that up as part of an explaination on how one could portray &quot;Arcadia in a different manner than the default. I also find it a tad insulting that, just because someone disagrees with you, they must be short sighted or disingenuous. You don't like what I'm saying, then focus on the words, not on calling me something. That's just rude.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The Gentry are only secondarily responsible for the fact that truth and inner nature are mutable in Arcadia.<br /> <br /> The Fae <i>are Faerie</i>, and thus any talk about changing the ontology of Arcadia must necessarily address their handling as well. </div> </div> </div>So... the Gentry are the same thing as Arcadia, and yet they are secondary? Contradicting yourself here! Either they're the same thing (and thus equal) or they're diffent, and one less than the other.<br /> <br /> And all that is immaterial. Its simplicity within itself to run the game with Arcadia NOT being like that, and the Keeper being the one with the power for the whole mutability. The core of the discussion is about how important Arcadia is to CtL. If we assume that its the Keeper, irregardless of the land,<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>ArcaneArts</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/513011-how-important-is-arcadia?p=530316#post530316\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Still don't...quite feel like responding to the major stuff, but I want to hit on something.<br /> <br /> Why metafiction, and why narrative theory, for this discussion?<br /> <br /> Changeling is a thematically multilayered game, but still:<br /> <br /> 1) This is still a storytelling game. While the storytelling aspect does not override the game, it is still a factor in it. The World of Darkness keeps a loose sense of narrative function for their elements, applying storytelling craft to gameplay. This is more important for Changeling (and Beast) than the others, because<br /> <br /> 2) This is a game about stories as much as anything else. It unravels storytelling and it's craft to examine it closely and challenge it, and uses it as a parallel to psychological horror and drama, ala Jung. It draws its' pedigree as much from Alan Wake and Princess Tutu as much as it does Grimm and Anderson. The True Fae <i>are</i> self-perpetuating stories. The Wyrd <i>is</i> essentially the force of narrative inspiration. They aren't <i>just</i> these things, but a lot of the horror of Changeling has to do with the horror of the conventions we take for granted in fiction, particularly fantasy and especially faerie tales. It blends this real world fears and problems and grounds itself out with logical conclusions, but it's involved enough that any discussion about Lost needs to keep in mind that we're talking about our relationship with fiction on top of everything else. Understanding the broad and many functions from a literary standpoint gives us a framework to understand everything else with clarity.<br /> <br /> I had more points when I started, but I lost them. God I'm tired.<br /> <br /> Unrelated Point: TREATISE! That was the word I was looking for, not thesis.<br /> <br /> Words are arbitrary, language is a lie, and English is god-awfully, lousy, and full of umlauts.</div> </div> </div> </div> The problem here is that<i> none of that is necessary to play the game. </i> Its missing the forest for the tress. The vast majority of people who sit down and play the game aren't going to be dissecting the narrative of the game into minute details. Discussing narrative theory can be fun at a meta-game level, but when we sit down to play, when the STs makes up their campaigns, generally they're not going to be considering theory. They will be looking at what will make an interesting story for their troupe to run through.<br /> <br /> So, the first questions we should be asking revolve around player perception, and how it relates to the story the ST wants to tell. I sit down with a story idea that involves Arcadia being entirely inside of dreams, and once you escape, you can never actually be taken back. The Keepers are still after you for various reasons and methods, but you'll never actually return to the same region you left before.<br /> <br /> From a character perspective, if my former Keeper is still after me for some reason, how important is the Arcadia bit on day to day gaming? Not very. The most important question here is &quot;Why should the characters care?&quot; If you have an awesome world built up, its not that important if you don't actually use it in play.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>CSSchulz</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/513011-how-important-is-arcadia?p=530643#post530643\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">I think the disagreement people are having is rooted in looking at the question &quot;How important is Arcadia?&quot; two different ways:</span></span></span></div> </div> </div> </div> Wise observation.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">1. How important is Arcadia <i>to the canon</i>? - I think everyone can agree that Arcadia is a major part of the lore of the setting as written. Apart from the difference of its absence, removing it will cause ripples that will affect many aspects of the game. Lots of questions that will have to be answered for the setting to make sense, or that will become mysteries (see my previous posts). Additionally, it will remove or alter certain thematic elements of the game that some people consider core to the games identity. (Personally, I think that other things could be used in its place to restore most of those thematic elements, but that's still more change.)</span></span></span> </div> </div> </div> Though, I still do disagree here. Every ST runs things differently; the games are designed such that the books do not give the answers, but rather expects the STs to have the flexibility to create their own relationships between Hedge, dreams, and Arcadia.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I think this is where MCN is coming from. </div> </div> </div> Yep. As I've said above, my experience has been that the Lost don't return to &quot;Arcadia&quot; in the vast majority of games I've seen and played in. So, its removal will only affect the minority games that care about going to Arcaida itself. The Deep Hedge and dreams are plenty strange if you need something off the wall for exploring.<br /> <br /> At best, its a bit of trivia for the Autumn to argue about until it comes up in plot.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2102,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2102-mcn","user":"MCN","date":". 05-30-2015, 05:25 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2102-mcn","name":"MCN","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2102_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3479"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-30T16:04:35-07:00","id":533739,"title":"","content":"Okay, I'm <i>just </i>tired enough of this thread to take the arguments I had and leave them for the Hedge Thread I'm starting tomorrow, so I'm going to reduce this to the simple points.<br /> <br /> 1)From what you've seen, is Dave and his crew going to remove Arcadia?<br /> <br /> (That's rhetorical-the answer is no. If you want to argue about how that's a bad decision, you may go ahead and do so, but it's not changing anything.)<br /> <br /> 2)Does that mean you have to use Arcadia?<br /> <br /> (That's also rhetorical-the answer is no. It's a toolbox world.)<br /> <br /> The OP asked if Arcadia was important. I think so, and I can sit here arguing with you all until I'm blue in the face about why. But really, it comes down this: that Onyx Path, and the people employed by them, thinks it is, and as far as canon goes, it is better to sit here and try and discover the reasons they think it is rather than all the reasons it's not. Put simply, &quot;Why?&quot; in this instance is more important than &quot;Is it or isn't it?&quot;<br /> <br /> If you want to deviate from the canon, then that is for each individual here to decide. I'm not going to tell you how to run your game.<br /> <br /> At this point, I'd rather just accept that I see something and others don't. That isn't an slap against anyone who doesn't agree with me, just an acknowledgment that some people have different viewpoints, and I'm not interested in making this the hill I fight tooth and nail on. I'd rather use the things I have to say in a positive sense than a negative one.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":99,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","user":"ArcaneArts","date":". 05-30-2015, 07:09 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-30T17:57:09-07:00","id":533825,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>ArcaneArts</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/513011-how-important-is-arcadia?p=533739#post533739\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Okay, I'm <i>just </i>tired enough of this thread to take the arguments I had and leave them for the Hedge Thread I'm starting tomorrow, so I'm going to reduce this to the simple points.<br /> <br /> 1)From what you've seen, is Dave and his crew going to remove Arcadia?<br /> <br /> (That's rhetorical-the answer is no. If you want to argue about how that's a bad decision, you may go ahead and do so, but it's not changing anything.)<br /> <br /> 2)Does that mean you have to use Arcadia?<br /> <br /> (That's also rhetorical-the answer is no. It's a toolbox world.)<br /> <br /> The OP asked if Arcadia was important. I think so, and I can sit here arguing with you all until I'm blue in the face about why. But really, it comes down this: that Onyx Path, and the people employed by them, thinks it is, and as far as canon goes, it is better to sit here and try and discover the reasons they think it is rather than all the reasons it's not. Put simply, &quot;Why?&quot; in this instance is more important than &quot;Is it or isn't it?&quot;<br /> <br /> If you want to deviate from the canon, then that is for each individual here to decide. I'm not going to tell you how to run your game.<br /> <br /> At this point, I'd rather just accept that I see something and others don't. That isn't an slap against anyone who doesn't agree with me, just an acknowledgment that some people have different viewpoints, and I'm not interested in making this the hill I fight tooth and nail on. I'd rather use the things I have to say in a positive sense than a negative one.</div> </div> </div> </div>For what it's worth, I was very much enjoying reading your treatises, importance of Arcadia aside, and eagerly look forward to your thread tomorrow. I've got no horse in the &quot;is Arcadia important?&quot; race, but you've got some fascinating things to say on the matter either way, so thank you for sharing them.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-30T18:08:45-07:00","id":533833,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>ArcaneArts</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/513011-how-important-is-arcadia?p=533739#post533739\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">1)From what you've seen, is Dave and his crew going to remove Arcadia?<br /> <br /> (That's rhetorical-the answer is no. If you want to argue about how that's a bad decision, you may go ahead and do so, but it's not changing anything.)</div> </div> </div> </div>Where did this come from? Maybe I misinterpreted this thread, but I didn't think we were ever talking about Arcadia being removed from the published books. I thought we were talking about removing Arcadia as a mod for an individual campaign/chronicle, not discussing whether it should stay part of the setting in 2e.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5910-csschulz","name":"CSSchulz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5910_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"177"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-30T18:28:22-07:00","id":533861,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MCN</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/513011-how-important-is-arcadia?p=533643#post533643\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> That's taking what I said out of context. Arcadia is most definitely portrayed as a specific place you travel to. One area of the Hedge cannot be &quot;Arcadia&quot; for Tom Riddle the Summer Beast, while not Arcadia Jem Synergy the Spring Fairest singer. If an area is Arcadia, its Arcadia for everyone. That's what I mean by objective.</div> </div> </div> </div>Who is escorting whom to Arcadia?<br /> <br /> The Hedge doesn't do consistent directions far from Earthside and Tom's Arcadia may well run down a completely different route than Jem's.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Care to back that up with actual source material? That sounds like pure speculation to me. </div> </div> </div>I struggle in vain to find instances of the Fae making a changeling of someone outside the defining context of their being brought to the seat of the Wyrd. (I trust I do not need to explain how the place that gives rise to countless diverging variants of Fae with dramatically different portfolios whose only common ground is &quot;cannot create cold iron&quot; is indeed endlessly variable.)<br /> <br /> The Thorns may tear your soul off of you, but the secondhand magic of the Hedge makes <i>hobgoblins</i> out of those cases and there exists no changeling on the books who was made what they are through action on the part of the Fae that did not bring them through Faerie.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> No, I brought that up as part of an explaination on how one could portray &quot;Arcadia in a different manner than the default. </div> </div> </div>There are at least two different theories presented in the books for &quot;Arcadia is not one specific place&quot; and the default presentation is by nature not thoroughly defined enough for those theories to warrant distinct assumptions about the nature of the metaphysics.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I also find it a tad insulting that, just because someone disagrees with you, they must be short sighted or disingenuous. You don't like what I'm saying, then focus on the words, not on calling me something. That's just rude.<br /> <br /> So... the Gentry are the same thing as Arcadia, and yet they are secondary? Contradicting yourself here! Either they're the same thing (and thus equal) or they're diffent, and one less than the other. </div> </div> </div>Taking this as a tally for myopia.<br /> <br /> The Gentry arise from Arcadia, provide the bulk of its definite physical laws while occupying the class of immortality that gives them individual will, and serve as the proxies through which the Lost gain the pledges that allow them to survive Arcadia.<br /> <br /> This does not change the fact that making the Strangers instead come from nowhere and require no such offer of hospitality to make someone a changeling is a change to both the Fae and Faerie, nor does it change the fact that the Keepers precipitating active changes to the fabric of Arcadia is a function of Arcadia being mutable by nature rather than the unqualified possession of reality-warping abilities by the Fae that just happen to work less effectively in dreams and the Hedge and less effectively still on Earth.<br /> <br /> If you're going to call people rude, playing &quot;gotcha&quot; is a bad followup.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":111,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","user":"Satchel","date":". 05-30-2015, 09:34 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-30T19:12:45-07:00","id":533892,"title":"","content":"<span style=\"color:red\">Let's try to bring this down a notch, please.</span>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-30T19:23:59-07:00","id":533900,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>CSSchulz</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/513011-how-important-is-arcadia?p=533833#post533833\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Where did this come from? Maybe I misinterpreted this thread, but I didn't think we were ever talking about Arcadia being removed from the published books. I thought we were talking about removing Arcadia as a mod for an individual campaign/chronicle, not discussing whether it should stay part of the setting in 2e.</div> </div> </div> </div>This came from me being a tired grumpy cat. Er, dog.<br /> <br /> I freely admit, the last post was a poopy-doo-doo response to the thread that basically says &quot;If you're sticking with canon, arguing against Arcadia is futile regardless of reason, and if you're not, then who cares, do what you want&quot;, which doesn't respond to <i>anything</i>. It's a deadening responses that contributes nothing.<br /> <br /> But somehow, in the course of a week, responding to this thread went, through the fault of no one in particular, from being a fun challenge to being a chore that reinforced problems with fandom as a concept in general that has been sitting on my head. MCN's response was kind of a final trigger for me because, as I briefly mentioned, a forest is made up of trees, and likewise a setting is made alive by the combined context of it's individual elements and the reasonings behind them.<br /> <br /> I don't like swinging it around in conversations like this, because any asshole can come in and say &quot;I've got a degree and you should listen to me&quot;, but as I mentioned I am a Creative Writing English major(this is going somewhere, no, it's not going where you think it is). I'm graduating this year. I have spent years learning to be a deep and critical reader and analyst as well as a craftsman of narrative. I aspire to be a multimedia storyteller, and I think I do fairly good at it. So it's frustrating to have people argue with me on subjects I take some time to enumerate on.<br /> <br /> That said, I didn't properly clarify on it at the beginning, because there are a lot of elements I take for granted as being accepted by the community. An example is how, as a game that is as much about the critical examination of escapism* as Dreaming is(just in a different direction) as anything else, I figured that it would be apparent how, for a protagonist type who's end-game of self-actualization involves rejecting the total dominance of escapist fantasy** over their lives and instead facing a world that doesn't play by reliable narrative rules(Yes, reliable, I mean that***) with courage and serenity in the face of the unknown, having an antagonist type who lives in a semi-shared semi-communal self-created world of whim-fulfillment and limited/controlled conflict is the inherent antithesis of said protagonists, and is therefore their final threshold towards self-actualization(if not self-transcendence) as the character overcomes the manifestation of their greatest temptation.<br /> <br /> I could sit here and go on about it, but (<b>here's where the point comes around</b>) at what point am I just clubbing you guys upside the head with the major I may or may not be lying about? I don't want to be the asshole who uses his education as a pillar of status-I'd rather be the guy who understands all this stuff so others don't have to, rather like Dave Brookshaw learning Plato so Mage fans don't have to. And for me, I was starting to get to the point where I was wanting to, effectively, eloquently, and intelligently just say &quot;SHUT UP, I KNOW WHAT I'M TALKING ABOUT AND YOU DON'T, SO NYEH!&quot;<br /> <br /> I was grumpy, but I recognized I was going to a bad place, so I did a limp dick move and bowed out.<br /> <br /> *Clarifying, I am speaking of, first, the niche part of any media consumer base that choose to indulge in their chosen product to escape the whole of their reality(In other words, I'm saying True Fae are those guys who let their babies die because they're too busy playing World of Warcraft), and second, the larger populace that perceive fiction as largely existing to serve only escapism and in fact perpetuate those ends(no analogue in the game, but the sheer fact that the game's protagonists reject letting fantasy define their lives is statement enough)<br /> <br /> **here pulling double duty as also a representation of a survivor learning to let go of their trauma-induced behaviors in the form of faerie tale tropes.<br /> <br /> ***I'll explain now, actually. As strange as the laws of the Fae are, there's a consistency to them, and once figured out, it's way simpler to know and work with than the thousand thousand variables of real life. I don't imagine you have to be a English Major to go sit in a movie and recognize within a few scenes the general gist of how it's working. You have no way of verifying the following, but I have gotten A's on papers talking about books I've never read just because I went and got the gist of a few chapters. In this sense, Fae rules are reliable, and if you don't believe narrative is a factor, may I remind you that, as a small sampling, that Talespinning is in fact a thing.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":99,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","user":"ArcaneArts","date":". 05-31-2015, 12:58 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-30T22:02:08-07:00","id":534010,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>ArcaneArts</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/513011-how-important-is-arcadia?p=533900#post533900\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> &quot;If you're sticking with canon, arguing against Arcadia is futile regardless of reason, and if you're not, then who cares, do what you want&quot;, which doesn't respond to <i>anything</i>. It's a deadening responses that contributes nothing.</div> </div> </div> </div>I had assumed from the get go that, for the purposes of this thread, we weren't sticking with canon, since removing Arcadia is obviously a deviation. Personally, I really enjoy exploring hypotheticals, so I guess I'm the answer to the question &quot;who cares&quot;, and I was going for &quot;this is how you might do it, if you wanted&quot; instead of &quot;do what you want.&quot;<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>ArcaneArts</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/513011-how-important-is-arcadia?p=533900#post533900\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">That said, I didn't properly clarify on it at the beginning, because there are a lot of elements I take for granted as being accepted by the community. An example is how, as a game that is as much about the critical examination of escapsim* as Dreaming is(just in a different direction) as anything else, I figured that it would be apparent how, for a protagonist type who's end-game of self-actualization involves rejecting the total dominance of escapist fantasy** over their lives and instead facing a world that doesn't play by reliable narrative rules(Yes, reliable, I mean that***) with courage and serenity in the face of the unknown, having an antagonist type who lives in a semi-shared semi-communal self-created world of whim-fulfillment and limited/controlled conflict is the inherent antithesis of said protagonists, and is therefore their final threshold towards self-actualization(if not self-transcendence) as the character overcomes the manifestation of their greatest temptation.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'd like to discuss this further, if you're not too fed up with the topic, but let me get some further clarification first. When you refer to the protagonist's &quot;end-game of self-actualization&quot; and the Keeper's role as &quot;their final threshold towards self-actualization&quot;, are you referring to the escape from the Durance or a future confrontation as a culmination of the game? If it is the latter, do you feel that all Changeling games must eventually lead to such a confrontation? Because I have to agree with other posters that most Changeling games I've been involved with haven't gone this way, and I still felt that they told satisfying, meaningful stories.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5910-csschulz","name":"CSSchulz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5910_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"177"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-31T04:25:17-07:00","id":534208,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Satchel</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/513011-how-important-is-arcadia?p=533861#post533861\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Who is escorting whom to Arcadia?<br /> <br /> The Hedge doesn't do consistent directions far from Earthside and Tom's Arcadia may well run down a completely different route than Jem's.</div> </div> </div> </div> If Tom and Jem are traveling together, then they both reach Arcadia at the same time. Tom doesn't reach an &quot;Arcadia&quot; space and Jem not if they're standing side-by-side.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I struggle in vain to find instances of the Fae making a changeling of someone outside the defining context of their being brought to the seat of the Wyrd. (I trust I do not need to explain how the place that gives rise to countless diverging variants of Fae with dramatically different portfolios whose only common ground is &quot;cannot create cold iron&quot; is indeed endlessly variable.) </div> </div> </div> Absence of evidence does not imply evidence of absence. Positive correlation does not imply cause and effect. The True Fae generally live in Arcadia, so that's where most of the changelings are transformed. That said, there's nothing to suggest that the Horned Hunter has to take the victims of the Wild Hunt to Faerie in order to transform them into his hounds.<br /> <br /> Secondly... the &quot;seat of the Wyrd?&quot; Where do you get that idea? We don't know what the Wyrd is in the first place, let alone where its &quot;seat&quot; is.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The Thorns may tear your soul off of you, but the secondhand magic of the Hedge makes <i>hobgoblins</i> out of those cases and there exists no changeling on the books who was made what they are through action on the part of the Fae that did not bring them through Faerie. </div> </div> </div> .... what? Where do you get the idea that people are being made into hobgoblins by virtue of Thorns tearing off the soul? I've don't recall anything to that effect.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Taking this as a tally for myopia. </div> </div> </div> You seem to have fixated on your view of how CtL runs as being the one and only way, and anyone who dissents must be wrong.<br /> <br /> The game is designed to be flexible enough that the ST gets to fill in what the Wyrd is, how things work, what Arcadia is. That's a feature of the game. Making connections and assumptions about what is or is not, or what should or should not, works for you. However, not everyone buys into the same worldview you have.<br /> <br /> There is no One True Way to look at things here. I'm taking issue with all these things you are saying because, frankly, they aren't the default game. You have a good idea of how the game works... <i>for you. </i>I'm seeing a lot of things that aren't part of the default game, I'm seeing a lot of assumptions. I don't know if we're dealing with group think, or circular logic, or something else here, but your ideas are not universally correct. You're not wrong, but you are not right for every game and every table either. There are other ways of looking at the available data from the books, other conclusions to draw.<br /> <br /> So, unless you have actual references from the books or from the developers and writers? You cannot say someone is right or wrong.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The Gentry arise from Arcadia, provide the bulk of its definite physical laws while occupying the class of immortality that gives them individual will, and serve as the proxies through which the Lost gain the pledges that allow them to survive Arcadia.<br /> <br /> This does not change the fact that making the Strangers instead come from nowhere and require no such offer of hospitality to make someone a changeling is a change to both the Fae and Faerie, nor does it change the fact that the Keepers precipitating active changes to the fabric of Arcadia is a function of Arcadia being mutable by nature rather than the unqualified possession of reality-warping abilities by the Fae that just happen to work less effectively in dreams and the Hedge and less effectively still on Earth. </div> </div> </div> The Gentry don't arise out of nowhere. Equinox Roads suggests a theme where they actually arise out of changelings that are low-Clarity and high Wyrd. Its entirely possible that's where they all came from. Your ideas seem to be a theory that you favor, not absolute fact. Its a wonderful theory.<br /> <br /> However, someone with a different idea of how the world works is not wrong either.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> If you're going to call people rude, playing &quot;gotcha&quot; is a bad followup. </div> </div> </div> I'm not playing gotcha, I'm saying that what you are saying makes no sense! You are saying two different things that make no sense when taken together! Maybe its an issue of miscommunication, or something.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2102,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2102-mcn","user":"MCN","date":". 05-31-2015, 07:29 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2102-mcn","name":"MCN","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2102_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3479"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-31T05:38:22-07:00","id":534231,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>ArcaneArts</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/513011-how-important-is-arcadia?p=533900#post533900\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So it's frustrating to have people argue with me on subjects I take some time to enumerate on.</div> </div> </div> </div> With all due respect, you are not one of the line writers or developers. No matter how well developed your arguments are, if I disagree with the very basis of your ideas, then I see no reason why I shouldn't argue it.<br /> <br /> You do have wonderful organization, ideas, and views on the subject. No arguments there. However, they're all from a meta-view of the world, not an in-game one. In a novel, I would say that dichotomy between the freehold and Faerie would be important. I do remember one of the writers, back with CtL first came out, mentioning that the Lost were the &quot;light fae,&quot; the good guys, the Seelie fae, where the Gentry were the dark Unseelie. I agree with that dichotomy.<br /> <br /> However, the narrative style of gaming makes such stories naturally different. We're dealing with an entirely multiple first-person perspective, and any specific location in the story is only as important as what the characters see and experience. The Gentry are the main antagonists, but the overwhelming majority of action takes place from the character's home, and their travels into the Hedge.<br /> <br /> I think that's important because it changes how much things matter. What matters is how the main characters see things - if they don't feel or see a dichotomy, or its importance? Then its not. I feel that their perception is far most important thing here, far more than the world design. Perception is very much reality in this case.<br /> <br /> Because of that perception, the very nature of things how we should classify things change. Because of that perception, the reality of what the True Fae represent may change from the Lost's opposites into something else. That's one reason why I think we're moving the main antagonists from the Gentry to the Huntsmen. The Fae are distant if powerful threats. Because they're distant, its less important to the narrative.<br /> <br /> You may know what you are talking about, but I feel like it has as much weight as a white-room theory craft. Make no mistake, they're fun to hash out. But when taken into the context of a game, everything changes. That's why I asked why were talking about from a theory perspective. Because I don't think that it matters as much as how it affects the story as much.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> That said, I didn't properly clarify on it at the beginning, because there are a lot of elements I take for granted as being accepted by the community. </div> </div> </div> I think that's another problem. Not everyone has the same view of how Changeling works. A number of your elements are things I just don't agree with being universally true. They do make sense, they fit very well. However, I don't think that we should be considering them the default. <br /> <br /> I see a lot of things being said that seem to be things assumed to be true when I don't think they should be.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2102,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2102-mcn","user":"MCN","date":". 05-31-2015, 08:41 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2102-mcn","name":"MCN","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2102_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3479"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-31T06:56:45-07:00","id":534280,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MCN</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/513011-how-important-is-arcadia?p=534208#post534208\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">.... what? Where do you get the idea that people are being made into hobgoblins by virtue of Thorns tearing off the soul? I've don't recall anything to that effect.</div> </div> </div> </div>The entry for Briar Wolves lists mortals getting lost in the Hedge and transformed as one of their possible origins. Autumn Nightmares also describes mundane creatures being warped by the Wyrd as one of a few possible ways that Hobgoblins can come to be.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5910-csschulz","name":"CSSchulz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5910_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"177"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-31T10:28:24-07:00","id":534581,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MCN</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/513011-how-important-is-arcadia?p=534208#post534208\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> If Tom and Jem are traveling together, then they both reach Arcadia at the same time. Tom doesn't reach an &quot;Arcadia&quot; space and Jem not if they're standing side-by-side.</div> </div> </div> </div>Reaching &quot;an&quot; Arcadia is not the same thing as reaching &quot;your&quot; Arcadia. Who's got the higher Wyrd rating or other narrative priority? Where along the continuum of Faerie are they going? Why are they going to Arcadia? One does not undertake a potentially fatal journey of that sort solely for the purpose of being a white-room example in an argument.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Absence of evidence does not imply evidence of absence. Positive correlation does not imply cause and effect. The True Fae generally live in Arcadia, so that's where most of the changelings are transformed. That said, there's nothing to suggest that the Horned Hunter has to take the victims of the Wild Hunt to Faerie in order to transform them into his hounds. </div> </div> </div>&quot;Forged from the shadows of his changeling prey&quot; suggests that the Hunter's hounds are a poor counterexample and the fact that the line's entire treatment on exiled Fae makes more reference to sending humans down to Faerie than it does to their making changelings of their own while away from the Jeweled Realm does more to suggest inability to do so than a bizarre consistency of Fae caprice.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Secondly... the &quot;seat of the Wyrd?&quot; Where do you get that idea? We don't know what the Wyrd is in the first place, let alone where its &quot;seat&quot; is. </div> </div> </div>We have a lexicon in the corebook where &quot;Wyrd&quot; is defined. That lexicon also loosely defines the Fae and Faerie in terms that place both in a centralizing or at worst near-core context relative to the Wyrd.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> .... what? Where do you get the idea that people are being made into hobgoblins by virtue of Thorns tearing off the soul? I've don't recall anything to that effect. </div> </div> </div>Three of the first four books in the line contain some material to that effect by virtue of their addressing hobgoblins and the Hedge. The Thorns tear the soul as a matter of definition, getting lost in the Hedge is seldom if ever framed in terms of staying on the path (since the only other places the paths go are back to Earth or onward to Faerie), and many hobgoblins are produced through exposure of something from Earth to the magic of the Hedge, including, as Schulz mentions, some classes of briarwolf.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The game is designed to be flexible enough that the ST gets to fill in what the Wyrd is, how things work, what Arcadia is. That's a feature of the game. Making connections and assumptions about what is or is not, or what should or should not, works for you. However, not everyone buys into the same worldview you have. </div> </div> </div>Again, there's a lexicon in the corebook that is borne out by the rest of the game's treatment of those terms.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> There is no One True Way to look at things here. I'm taking issue with all these things you are saying because, frankly, they aren't the default game. You have a good idea of how the game works... <i>for you. </i>I'm seeing a lot of things that aren't part of the default game, I'm seeing a lot of assumptions. I don't know if we're dealing with group think, or circular logic, or something else here, but your ideas are not universally correct. </div> </div> </div>I would prefer not to bring out the &quot;read the books&quot; sign in the face of yet another accusation of egotism.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> You're not wrong, but you are not right for every game and every table either. </div> </div> </div>I'm aware of Rule Zero and its neighboring conventions. &quot;You can do what you want at your table&quot; obviates the entire point of discussing this topic outside the context of how to implement the subject proposal in a specific game.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> So, unless you have actual references from the books or from the developers and writers? You cannot say someone is right or wrong. </div> </div> </div>My antipathy for the Listed Citation War is hard-won and partially founded on concern for volumetric copyright and the inconvenience of losing bus space, but fine, if you want to play that card, state the particular thing you want proof of and I'll get digging.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The Gentry don't arise out of nowhere. Equinox Roads suggests a theme where they actually arise out of changelings that are low-Clarity and high Wyrd. Its entirely possible that's where they all came from. </div> </div> </div>The corebook, Autumn Nightmares, and Equinox Road itself are fairly explicit that humanity is not the sole source of the Fae, and said &quot;theme&quot; is part of a system where the tipping point of Becoming Nightmare is <i>running off to Faerie to complete the transformation.</i><br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I'm not playing gotcha, I'm saying that what you are saying makes no sense! You are saying two different things that make no sense when taken together! Maybe its an issue of miscommunication, or something. </div> </div> </div>Imperfect metaphor inbound:<br /> <br /> If lava monsters are born from a giant magical lava pit whose contents a) can be controlled by the lava monsters and b) make captured humans malleable enough to become half lava monster if they are adapted to take sustenance from magical lava, then it is not contradictory to say that removing the pit a) necessitates changes to how the lava monsters work and b) removes the means by which the lava monsters can make a captured human half lava monster.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":111,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","user":"Satchel","date":". 05-31-2015, 04:26 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-31T10:28:37-07:00","id":534582,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>CSSchulz</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/513011-how-important-is-arcadia?p=534280#post534280\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The entry for Briar Wolves lists mortals getting lost in the Hedge and transformed as one of their possible origins. Autumn Nightmares also describes mundane creatures being warped by the Wyrd as one of a few possible ways that Hobgoblins can come to be.</div> </div> </div> </div> Mundane critters becoming hobs I'm familiar with, but in either case, it doesn't seem to suggest that the Thorns are the guilty party for humans turning into briarwolves. In no part does it say that you need to have any of your soul lost in order to become a briarwolf, assuming that's true. Certainly, its not enough to definitively say that humans lost in the Hedge, but never into Arcadia, definitely become hobs. One group, sure. Everyone? Based on a story they tell each other about the Huntsman's favored pets? <br /> <br /> The Huntsman story, where the targets of the Wild Hunt become the hounds inside of the Hunt, is a standard story in legend. Not only is this repeated in The Mad Hound and The Huntsman writeup in the antagonist section, but it implies that it has to do with the magic of the briarwolves themselves instead of the Thorns. It also could qualify as one of the Gentry who can make people into changelings without being in Arcadia proper, but the stories in the book are flexible enough that it could require going back there too.<br /> <br /> I'm familiar with non-human critters becoming hobs, but that could easily be what happens with any non-human - kind of like how ghoul animals and mandrakes follow different rules than human ghouls. Its pretty standard in WoD for humans to react differently to magic than to other animals. As far as I'm aware, no animal can qualify for any of the part-changeling merits in Equinox Roads either.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2102-mcn","name":"MCN","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2102_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3479"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}